pgbench Standard Benchmark Workload Suites
==========================================

This directory contains ready-to-run workload suites modeled after and inspired
by standard industry database benchmarks, implemented using pgbench's scripting
features (\try ... \catch, \sleep, \if, \gset, \gset_meta, --group, --sla,
--json-report, and the contrib/pgbench SQL helper extension):

  pgbench-b/     pgbench-b (modernized database-function based banking workload)
  pgbench-c/     pgbench-c (TPC-C-inspired OLTP order-entry workload)
  pgbench-e/     pgbench-e (TPC-E-inspired financial brokerage OLTP workload)
  pgbench-h/     pgbench-h (TPC-H-inspired decision support / ad-hoc analytical queries + refresh)
  pgbench-ds/    pgbench-ds (TPC-DS-inspired multi-channel retail decision support + maintenance)
  pgbench-htap/  pgbench-htap (CH-benCHmark-inspired hybrid HTAP combining pgbench-c OLTP + analytical OLAP)

Trademark and Naming Note:
--------------------------
These workload suites are inspired by and modeled after specifications published
by the Transaction Processing Performance Council (TPC) and community hybrid
benchmarks (CH-benCHmark). To respect and avoid infringing upon TPC trademarks
(such as TPC-C, TPC-E, TPC-H, and TPC-DS), and to clearly distinguish these
community engineering tools from official, council-audited benchmark results,
these suites are named pgbench-c, pgbench-e, pgbench-h, pgbench-ds, and
pgbench-htap. Results generated by these scripts represent un-audited internal
performance measurements and must not be characterized as official TPC-certified
ratings.

All five suites generate initial data entirely in the database using
generate_series() and deterministic pseudo-random functions from the
contrib/pgbench extension (pgbench.random, pgbench.random_gaussian,
pgbench.random_exponential, pgbench.random_zipfian, pgbench.nurand,
pgbench.c_last, pgbench.random_astring, pgbench.random_nstring,
pgbench.date_add, pgbench.int_to_date, pgbench.format_string,
pgbench.permute, pgbench.hash). No external data generator binaries or flat
files are required.


Parallel In-Database Initialization
-----------------------------------

Each suite separates initialization into three phases:

  1. <suite>_schema.sql       DDL (tables, primary keys, and stored procedures)
  2. <suite>_init.sql         Data population partitioned across workers
  3. <suite>_constraints.sql  Secondary indexes, foreign keys, and ANALYZE

In <suite>_init.sql, data generation is partitioned across pgbench clients
using the built-in :client_id variable and -D num_workers=<N>:

  * Shared reference tables (e.g., item, nation, region, date_dim) are either
    loaded by client 0 (\if :client_id == 0) or partitioned by row ID modulo
    :num_workers (id % :num_workers = :client_id).
  * Scale-partitioned hierarchies (e.g., warehouses and their child stock,
    district, customer, and order rows) are partitioned by parent key modulo
    :num_workers ((w_id - 1) % :num_workers = :client_id) so workers never
    contend on the same partition.

To initialize with 4 parallel workers at scale 4:

  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_schema.sql
  pgbench -n -c 4 -j 4 -t 1 -D scale=4 -D num_workers=4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_init.sql
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_constraints.sql

If -D num_workers is omitted, num_workers defaults to 1 (single-client load).
During automated testing or quick functional verification, small density
overrides (such as -D items_per_scale=100 -D cust_per_dist=20) can be supplied
to both <suite>_init.sql and the transaction scripts.


0. pgbench-b (workloads/pgbench-b/)
-----------------------------------

Modeled after the classic Wisconsin banking benchmark specification and pgbench's
original embedded banking transactions, but modernized to execute all transaction
operations inside server-side database stored procedures (pgbench_b_transaction
and pgbench_b_simple_update). This eliminates multi-roundtrip network latency,
exercises PostgreSQL's stored procedure engine, and runs cleanly under default
autocommit mode without client-side transaction overhead.

Files:
  pgbench_b_schema.sql         Standard tables (pgbench_branches, tellers, accounts,
                               history) + stored procedures (pgbench_b_transaction,
                               pgbench_b_simple_update)
  pgbench_b_init.sql           Parallel in-database data generator partitioned across
                               workers using generate_series() and contrib/pgbench
  pgbench_b_constraints.sql    Primary keys, foreign keys, and ANALYZE
  pgbench_b_tx.sql             Standard transaction via CALL pgbench_b_transaction(...)
  pgbench_b_simple_update.sql  Simple-Update transaction via CALL pgbench_b_simple_update(...)
  pgbench_b_select_only.sql    Select-Only query against pgbench_accounts

Initialize:
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-b/pgbench_b_schema.sql
  pgbench -n -c 4 -j 4 -t 1 -D scale=10 -D num_workers=4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-b/pgbench_b_init.sql
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-b/pgbench_b_constraints.sql

Run standard transaction workload:
  pgbench -n -c 16 -j 4 -T 60 -D scale=10 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-b/pgbench_b_tx.sql


1. pgbench-c (workloads/pgbench-c/)
-----------------------------------

Modeled after the TPC-C order-entry OLTP workload specification.

Files:
  pgbench_c_schema.sql        9 tables (warehouse, district, customer, history,
                              new_order, orders, order_line, item, stock) and 5
                              stored procedures (pgbench_c_new_order,
                              pgbench_c_payment, pgbench_c_order_status,
                              pgbench_c_delivery, pgbench_c_stock_level)
  pgbench_c_init.sql          Parallel data generator (scale = warehouse count)
  pgbench_c_constraints.sql   Secondary indexes, foreign keys, and ANALYZE
  pgbench_c_new_order.sql     New-Order transaction (~45% mix, 1% simulated
                              invalid-item rollback caught via \try ... \catch,
                              with \keying_time and \think_time)
  pgbench_c_payment.sql       Payment transaction (~43% mix, 60% by last name via
                              pgbench.c_last(pgbench.nurand(255, 0, 999)))
  pgbench_c_order_status.sql  Order-Status read-only transaction (~4% mix)
  pgbench_c_delivery.sql      Delivery batch transaction (~4% mix using \for)
  pgbench_c_stock_level.sql   Stock-Level read-only scan (~4% mix)

Initialize (4 warehouses, 4 parallel loader threads):
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_schema.sql
  pgbench -n -c 4 -j 4 -t 1 -D scale=4 -D num_workers=4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_init.sql
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_constraints.sql

Run standard mix with SLA targets and JSON report:
  pgbench -n -c 16 -j 4 -T 60 -D scale=4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_new_order.sql@45 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_payment.sql@43 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_order_status.sql@4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_delivery.sql@4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-c/pgbench_c_stock_level.sql@4 \
          --sla="total:p95<=5000ms" \
          --json-report=pgbench_c_results.json


2. pgbench-e (workloads/pgbench-e/)
-----------------------------------

Modeled after the TPC-E financial brokerage OLTP benchmark specification.

Files:
  pgbench_e_schema.sql             16 brokerage tables and 10 stored procedures
  pgbench_e_init.sql               Parallel brokerage data generator
  pgbench_e_constraints.sql        Secondary indexes, foreign keys, and ANALYZE
  pgbench_e_trade_order.sql        Trade-Order write transaction (with 1% rollback)
  pgbench_e_trade_result.sql       Trade-Result write + Market-Feed update
  pgbench_e_customer_position.sql  Customer-Position + Security-Detail read
  pgbench_e_market_watch.sql       Market-Watch read
  pgbench_e_broker_volume.sql      Broker-Volume aggregation read
  pgbench_e_trade_status.sql       Trade-Status + Trade-Lookup read

Initialize (scale 2, 2 parallel loader threads):
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_schema.sql
  pgbench -n -c 2 -j 2 -t 1 -D scale=2 -D num_workers=2 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_init.sql
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_constraints.sql

Run brokerage mix:
  pgbench -n -c 8 -j 4 -T 60 -D scale=2 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_trade_order.sql@10 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_trade_result.sql@10 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_customer_position.sql@13 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_market_watch.sql@18 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_broker_volume.sql@5 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-e/pgbench_e_trade_status.sql@44


3. pgbench-h (workloads/pgbench-h/)
-----------------------------------

Modeled after the TPC-H decision support benchmark specification.

Files:
  pgbench_h_schema.sql       8 tables (region, nation, supplier, customer, part,
                             partsupp, orders, lineitem)
  pgbench_h_init.sql         Parallel decision support data generator
  pgbench_h_constraints.sql  Secondary indexes, foreign keys, and ANALYZE
  pgbench_h_q1.sql           Pricing Summary Report Query (Q1)
  pgbench_h_q3.sql           Shipping Priority Query (Q3)
  pgbench_h_q5.sql           Local Supplier Volume Query (Q5)
  pgbench_h_q6.sql           Forecasting Revenue Change Query (Q6)
  pgbench_h_q10.sql          Returned Item Reporting Query (Q10)
  pgbench_h_q12.sql          Shipping Modes and Order Priority Query (Q12)
  pgbench_h_q14.sql          Promotion Effect Query (Q14)
  pgbench_h_q19.sql          Discounted Revenue Query (Q19)
  pgbench_h_queries.sql      Combined multi-query stream (Q1..Q19)
  pgbench_h_refresh.sql      RF1/RF2 refresh stream (INSERT + DELETE pair)

Initialize:
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-h/pgbench_h_schema.sql
  pgbench -n -c 4 -j 4 -t 1 -D scale=1 -D num_workers=4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-h/pgbench_h_init.sql
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-h/pgbench_h_constraints.sql

Run concurrent query streams and refresh stream in separate client groups:
  pgbench -n -T 60 -D scale=1 \
          --group="name=queries,clients=4,threads=4,sequential=true,file=workloads/pgbench-h/pgbench_h_queries.sql" \
          --group="name=refresh,clients=1,threads=1,file=workloads/pgbench-h/pgbench_h_refresh.sql,rate=2"


4. pgbench-ds (workloads/pgbench-ds/)
-------------------------------------

Modeled after the TPC-DS multi-channel retail decision support benchmark specification.

Files:
  pgbench_ds_schema.sql          13 star-schema fact/dimension tables (date_dim,
                                 item, customer_address, customer_demographics,
                                 customer, store, warehouse, promotion, store_sales,
                                 store_returns, catalog_sales, web_sales, inventory)
  pgbench_ds_init.sql            Parallel star-schema data generator
  pgbench_ds_constraints.sql     Secondary indexes, foreign keys, and ANALYZE
  pgbench_ds_q_store_sales.sql   Store sales & returns rollup with CTE
  pgbench_ds_q_cross_channel.sql Cross-channel (store/catalog/web) window ranking
  pgbench_ds_q_inventory.sql     Warehouse inventory variation analysis
  pgbench_ds_q_promotion.sql     Promotional sales ratio analysis
  pgbench_ds_maintenance.sql     Fact table maintenance (insert/delete)

Initialize:
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_schema.sql
  pgbench -n -c 4 -j 4 -t 1 -D scale=1 -D num_workers=4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_init.sql
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_constraints.sql

Run reporting queries alongside maintenance:
  pgbench -n -T 60 -D scale=1 \
          --group="name=reporting,clients=4,threads=4,file=workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_q_store_sales.sql@25,file=workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_q_cross_channel.sql@25,file=workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_q_inventory.sql@25,file=workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_q_promotion.sql@25" \
          --group="name=maint,clients=1,threads=1,file=workloads/pgbench-ds/pgbench_ds_maintenance.sql,rate=2"


5. pgbench-htap (workloads/pgbench-htap/)
-----------------------------------------

Modeled after the CH-benCHmark hybrid transactional / analytical processing
(HTAP) benchmark specification, combining the operational schema of pgbench-c
with analytical dimensions and complex multi-table analytical queries.

Files:
  pgbench_htap_schema.sql       pgbench-c operational tables/procedures + HTAP analytical
                                dimension tables (htap_region, htap_nation, htap_supplier)
  pgbench_htap_init.sql         Parallel loader for combined operational + analytical
                                schema
  pgbench_htap_constraints.sql  Secondary indexes, foreign keys, and ANALYZE
  pgbench_htap_oltp.sql         Weighted pgbench-c OLTP stream (New-Order, Payment,
                                Order-Status, Delivery, Stock-Level)
  pgbench_htap_olap_q1.sql      HTAP Q1 (order_line pricing summary)
  pgbench_htap_olap_q3.sql      HTAP Q3 (unshipped order revenue priority)
  pgbench_htap_olap_q5.sql      HTAP Q5 (regional supplier revenue)
  pgbench_htap_olap_q6.sql      HTAP Q6 (forecasting revenue change)
  pgbench_htap_olap_q18.sql     HTAP Q18 (large volume customer query)

Initialize:
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_schema.sql
  pgbench -n -c 4 -j 4 -t 1 -D scale=4 -D num_workers=4 \
          -f workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_init.sql
  pgbench -n -f workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_constraints.sql

Run simultaneous OLTP and OLAP client groups with independent SLA targets:
  pgbench -n -T 60 -D scale=4 \
          --group="name=oltp,clients=8,threads=4,file=workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_oltp.sql" \
          --group="name=olap,clients=2,threads=2,file=workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_olap_q1.sql@20,file=workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_olap_q3.sql@20,file=workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_olap_q5.sql@20,file=workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_olap_q6.sql@20,file=workloads/pgbench-htap/pgbench_htap_olap_q18.sql@20" \
          --sla="oltp:p95<=5000ms" \
          --sla="olap:p95<=10000ms" \
          --json-report=htap_results.json
